            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Overall, your house has a lot of excess air leakage, which is the largest portion of the homes heating demand, approximately 36%. There are some key areas where air leakage occurs. Air leakage was observed through electrical outlets on your exterior walls and the weather stripping along the exterior doors



** Coming from the wood stove (through the flue). If possible, I recommend having a deticated comb air pipe installed.



** Around ceiling penetrations, such as light fixtures.



** The wall plugs &amp; switches in exterior walls, as well as the frame around the back exterior door is allowing air leakage &amp; heat loss. There is also air leakage along the header spaces. 



** Some of the windows do not close properly (paticularily those in the upstaits room above the garage). 



** There was quite a bit of air leakage observed around the window within the back door. I suspect the seal between the window and door has failed. 



Savings: For a modest 10% reduction in air leakage, you could potentially save $ 270 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 7.9 GJ, and qualify for a $500 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), if you complete an eligible wall insulation upgrade.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation, as well as 2x2 strapping. I have modelled adding 2 inches (R10) of extruded polystyrene insulation to all exterior walls of the home (this includes the adjoining walls between the home/garage).



Savings:

By increasing your walls by a minimum R10, you could potentially save $ 320 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 9.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 2,250 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), 



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</mainWalls>
                <foundation>

I have modelled increasing the basement (and crawlspace walls) by R10. 



I have also modelled adding 2 inches (R10) of extruded polystyrene insulation to the pony walls, in conjuction with the main walls.



Savings:

By increasing your basement walls by a minimum R10, you could potentially save $ 340 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 10.0 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 937.50 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), 



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</foundation>
                <windows>

As all of your windows are older wood framed triple pane ones that do appear to have a Low E coating or an insulating gas fill between the panes of glass. 

I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all of the windows in the home with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified window rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



Savings: 

By replacing the wood framed windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially save $ 110 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 3.3 GJ.  Windows that are ENERGY STAR?????? qualified and rated for climate zone 3 are eligible for $ 250 per window in rebates, to a max of 12 windows through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program. 



Although the savings above from replacing the windows appears minimal, this does not include the air sealing benefit. As many of the windows do not seal very well, this will greatly reduce the air leakage. 



To be eligible for a windows rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</windows>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil fired boiler was originally rated at 85% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. 



As you are currently using the boiler to heat your domestic hot water with a deticated loop, the savings below include the savings associated with the hot water using the 'new' boiler. 



Savings:

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, and continuing to use the boiler to heat your hot water, you could potentially save $ 2,100 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 50.8 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), 



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

WOODSTOVE

Savings (with current oil furnace):

By using your current wood stove for 50% of your heating load, you could potentially save $ 1,800 per year in energy savings. This assumes a cost of $ 100 per cord of wood for harvesting your own. 

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



ECM PUMPS

Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ?????????learn????????? the system?????????s usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house, you can qualify for $ 175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



FUEL COSTS

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $17.81 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.29 per litre 

Propane - $0.80 per litre

Wood - $341.25 per cord 

Pellets - $5.51 per bag

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



DISCLAIMER

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



YOUR ENERGY ADVISOR

Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Michael Goodwin

Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: michael.goodwin@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!

</AdditionalComments>
